WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Imagine two counters at a campus coffee
shop.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.750
Both take four minutes per customer on
average.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.650
At one counter, some orders are quick and
others take longer.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:13.250
At the other, every order takes exactly
four minutes.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.210
Which line would you join?

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:19.360
In Operations Management, this is a
waiting lines problem.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.420
Let's work through it using a bank teller
and a self-service machine.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.380
Customers arrive randomly at the bank at
an average rate of twelve per hour.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:33.350
Both the teller and the machine serve
fifteen customers per hour on average.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.330
The teller’s service time varies.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.560
The machine takes exactly four minutes
every time.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.640
Let’s follow one customer.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.830
Imagine they join each line at minute
three.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.160
The machine finishes serving the previous
customer at minute four,

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.880
so they wait one minute.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.740
The teller finishes at minute six,

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.470
so they wait three minutes.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:56.740
That’s one example of how a long
transaction can hold up the line.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.480
Now let’s calculate the long-run averages.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.160
First, find utilization.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.100
That’s the fraction of time each server is
busy.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.180
Divide twelve by fifteen to get zero point
eight.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.180
Both servers are busy eighty percent of
the time.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.190
For the teller, the average wait in line
is called W sub Q.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.270
Start with the gap between the service
rate and the arrival rate.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:20.720
Fifteen minus twelve is three.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.550
Multiply that gap by the service rate,
fifteen.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.270
Then divide the arrival rate, twelve, by
that product.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.170
The result is in hours.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.300
Multiply by sixty to get sixteen minutes.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:37.040
For the machine, the formula has an extra
factor of two in the denominator.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.350
So divide sixteen by two.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.540
The average wait is eight minutes.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.090
In this example, keeping service time
constant cuts the average wait in line in
half.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.660
Time in line ends when service begins.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.230
Time in the system includes service.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.990
Add four minutes of average service time
to each wait.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.410
That gives twenty minutes total for the
teller

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.500
and twelve minutes total for the machine.
